===Etymology 2===
From {{inh|en|enm|howe}}, {{m|enm|hough}}, {{m|enm|hogh}}, partly from {{inh|en|ang|hōh|t=promontory}}, and partly from {{der|en|non|haugr|t=a how, mound}}. Compare {{cog|fro|höe|t=hillock, hill}}, from the same {{cog|gem}} source.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|howe}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|dialectal}} An artificial [[barrow]] or [[tumulus]]; in later folklore, associated with [[fairy|fairies]].
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1928|author=w:Lewis Spence|title=Mysteries of Britain|page=v. 136|passage=Fianlly, as regards the places in which these rites and mysteries may have been held, certain writers believe them to have been the "Fairy Hills" or "'''howes'''" in various parts of Scotland.}}
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1985|author=Jennifer Westwood|title=Albion|year_published=1992|page=xi. 432|passage=The fairy feast inside the '''howe''' (and this barrow is hill-sized) reminds one of nothing so much as a tale in the Icelandic ''Eyrbyggia saga'', written in the mid thirteenth century.}}
# {{lb|en|dialectal}} A small hill in northern England.

